virglrenderer: uniform block arrays are named and bound as declared

What: a shader that indexes its uniform blocks at run time (CONST[ADDR + n]) gets
them as one GLSL array. That array now starts at the lowest block the shader
declares and runs to the highest, holes included; the renderer names, binds and
checks every element from the same mask, and the run-time index is
"addr + n - first" (it ignored n). Every element keeps the first block's size, as
before (the array has one element type). Before, the array had one element per declared
block, but the renderer named element k after block k + 1: with holes some names
did not exist (the block then counted as inactive) and some elements were never
bound. A second line of defence: before vrend binds a program's blocks, each
active block points at a binding vrend never fills; any block still there
afterwards (one vrend does not know by name) skips the program's draws.
Why: an element that vrend never bound stays on binding 0, whatever buffer
another stage put there. The GPU then reads the block from a buffer the uniform
block checks never saw (review of gpu-robust, repro 3: CONST[1] and CONST[3]
read 64 bytes from the vertex shader's 32-byte buffer). Mesa declares block
arrays without holes from the first block, so its shaders are unchanged.
Depends on: virgl-uniform-buffer-checks.patch, virgl-shader-index-clamp.patch.
Upstream: upstreamable fix of the block array naming; not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-gpu-ranges.c cases 33 (a block array with a hole
without a buffer: skipped) and 34 (all bound: drawn, the pixel shows the block
the index picked).
